# Structure and format of our config file

- WIDTH: Maze width (number of cells)
- HEIGHT: Maze height (number of cells)
- ENTRY: Entry coordinates x,y
- EXIT: Exit coordinates x,y
- OUTPUT_FILE: Output filename
- PERFECT: Is the maze perfect? True or False


# Maze generation algorithm

We use the random cell merging algorithm:

- Each cell has a unique identifier at the start.

- Walls between two cells are randomly knocked down if they do not have the same identifier.

- Once the wall has been knocked down, the two cells (and their respective groups) merge under the same identifier.

![Alt text](readme_pictures/Yl_maze_ani_algo1.gif)


# Maze solver algorithm:
We choose the Breadth-First-Search algorithm to find the solution of the maze because it find inevitably the shortest path between the entry and the exit of a maze.

To do so it explore all cells close to the current cell and save the previous visited cells until he find the exit.

On the other side, it cost a lot of memory depending to the maze size.

![Alt text](readme_pictures/BFS-Algorithm_Search_Way.gif)


# why this algorithm

## Breadth-First-Search algorithm

This algorithm ensures that we find the shortest path without risking loops in cases where the maze is imperfect and contains loops of cells.

## algorithme de fusion aleatoire de chemins

We chose this algorithm because it is easy to apply.

Furthermore, it allows us to be sure that the maze generated will be perfect because it stops when all the cells have been opened and does not open any walls if two cells are already connected.

# What part of our code is reusable and how ?

The Cell and Maze classes are designed to be reusable in other contexts by simply modifying the input parameters.

